WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.626
The payments table is split across four shards by payment id.

2
00:00:06.626 --> 00:00:10.810
The fraud check needs every payment card F made in the last hour.

3
00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.707
They were placed by id, so they're scattered.

4
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:18.312
Finding rows by card needs an index on card, a secondary index.

5
00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:20.429
Where should its entries live?

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.240
The simplest answer: each shard indexes its own rows.

7
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.669
That's a local index.

8
00:00:25.669 --> 00:00:30.637
A payment and its index entry go to the same shard, so they always agree.

9
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:34.592
But shard 1's index knows nothing about shard 3's rows.

10
00:00:34.592 --> 00:00:39.785
So the fraud check has to ask all four shards, even shard 2, which has none.

11
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:41.995
Then it waits for the slowest.

12
00:00:41.995 --> 00:00:44.429
Three answer in 2 or 3 milliseconds.

13
00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:47.944
This time one takes 50, so the whole check takes 50.

14
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:55.915
If each shard is slow one time in a hundred, a check that asks ten shards waits on a slow one about one time in ten.

15
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.709
Turn the index around.

16
00:00:57.709 --> 00:01:01.440
A global index is split by card, not by payment id.

17
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.952
All of card F's entries live on one index shard.

18
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:11.497
Now the check asks that one index shard, gets three payment ids, and fetches just those rows.

19
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.739
The cost moves to the write.

20
00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:21.676
A new payment for card F lands on row shard 2, but its index entry belongs on index shard 1, another machine.

21
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:28.325
Stores like DynamoDB update that index in the background, after the write has already succeeded.

22
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:32.767
So a check that runs right after the payment reads the index too early.

23
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:35.629
It finds three payments, and misses the new one.

24
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:40.491
A fraction of a second later the entry arrives, and the next check sees all four.

25
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:45.605
A local index stays in step with its rows, but every read asks every shard.

26
00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:50.280
A global index answers from one shard, but it can lag behind the write.
